      {
        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 4,
        "titulo": "Common mistakes and sentences in the past",
        "explicacao": "The mistakes English speakers WILL make in the past. Mistake 1: using the present, or looking for a person ending. ❌ Wrong: Я чита́ю вчера́. ✅ Right: Я чита́л вчера́. (a man) / Я чита́ла вчера́. (a woman). English speakers instinctively think \"I read\" and look for an \"I\" form of the past. It does not exist. The Russian past has no person. It has gender. Mistake 2: using -л for a woman. ❌ Wrong: Она́ чита́л вчера́. ✅ Right: Она́ чита́ла вчера́. If the subject is a woman, the ending is -ла, always. Mistake 3: using -л for a plural. ❌ Wrong: Они́ чита́л вчера́. ✅ Right: Они́ чита́ли вчера́. If the subject is plural, the ending is -ли, always, whatever the gender. Mistake 4: thinking that «я был» means \"I am\". ❌ Wrong: Я был брази́лец, meaning \"I am Brazilian\". ✅ Right: Я брази́лец (present, with no verb). Я был брази́лец (past: I was Brazilian). «Я был» means \"I was\". It never means \"I am\". For \"I am\", the verb does not appear: «я брази́лец». Mistake 5: not changing the form of быть for a woman. If you are a woman, never say «я был». Say «я была́». Always. Mistake 6: forgetting that идти becomes шёл. ❌ Wrong: Я идти́л вчера́. (It does not exist.) ✅ Right: Я шёл вчера́. (I was going yesterday.) The verb идти is irregular. The stem changes completely in the past. Mistake 7: using your own gender when you ask someone else. ❌ Wrong: A man asks a woman «Ты чита́л?» (using -л because he is a man). ✅ Right: A man asks a woman «Ты чита́ла?» (using -ла because she is a woman). This is the subtlest mistake. English speakers tend to use their own gender ending when asking a question, because in English the speaker's sex never affects the verb. But the ending follows the subject, not the person asking. If a man asks a woman, he uses -ла (because she is a woman). If a woman asks a man, she uses -л (because he is a man). Sentences in the past. Now real sentences, with adverbs of time. Notice the translations: all of them describe actions in progress or repeated. PART 2: THE FUTURE.",
